        <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Picking the right van for the job</h2>
          <p>
            A van that's too small wastes time on extra trips, while a van that's too big can feel tight in city streets and use extra fuel. Think carefully about:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Load length and height:</strong> Make sure long materials, racking, or tall items fit comfortably with the doors shut.</li>
            <li><strong>Payload weight:</strong> Factor in the weight of tools, stock, passengers, and any conversions—not just the empty van.</li>
            <li><strong>Access and parking:</strong> Check roof height limits for multi-storey car parks or height barriers on regular routes.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why leasing works well for vans</h2>
          <p>
            Vans work hard every day, so driving a newer model with manufacturer warranty cover helps keep downtime low. Leasing also lets businesses scale up or down as workloads change without locking huge sums of capital into depreciating vehicles.
          </p>
